"""You can see the description of the task using the code specified in the title on leetcode.
This program has O((2 ** n) * n) complexity by time and O((2 ** n) * n) by space.

This problem is beyond my capabilities. 
A simple brute-force approach won't pass due to complexity constraints (2^n), 
and I’m not capable of coming up with a more sophisticated solution within an hour.

The correct approach is backtracking with constraints. 
First, perform a single pass to count how many opening and closing parentheses violate the balance; 
this represents the minimum number of removals required. 
Then, generate solutions recursively: at each step, either remove the current element or keep it and proceed. 
Removals are permitted only until the limit is exhausted, ensuring that exactly the minimum number of characters is removed. 
A closing parenthesis may be kept only if there is a corresponding unmatched opening parenthesis; 
this prunes invalid branches immediately, without needing to process the string to the very end. 
If the removal limit reaches zero by the end of the string, the expression is valid and is added to the result set; 
a set is used because different removal sequences can yield identical strings.
"""

https://leetcode.com/problems/remove-invalid-parentheses/editorial/?envType=daily-question&envId=2026-10-07
class Solution:
    def removeInvalidParentheses(self, s):
        left = 0
        right = 0
        for char in s:
            if char == '(':
                left += 1
            elif char == ')':
                right = right + 1 if left == 0 else right
                left = left - 1 if left > 0 else left

        result = {}


        def recurse(s, index, left_count, right_count, left_rem, right_rem, expr):
            if index == len(s):
                if left_rem == 0 and right_rem == 0:
                    ans = "".join(expr)
                    result[ans] = 1
            else:
                if (s[index] == '(' and left_rem > 0) or (s[index] == ')' and right_rem > 0):
                    recurse(s, index + 1,
                            left_count,
                            right_count,
                            left_rem - (s[index] == '('),
                            right_rem - (s[index] == ')'), expr)
                expr.append(s[index])
                if s[index] != '(' and s[index] != ')':
                    recurse(s, index + 1,
                            left_count,
                            right_count,
                            left_rem,
                            right_rem, expr)
                elif s[index] == '(':
                    recurse(s, index + 1,
                            left_count + 1,
                            right_count,
                            left_rem,
                            right_rem, expr)
                elif s[index] == ')' and left_count > right_count:
                    recurse(s, index + 1,
                            left_count,
                            right_count + 1,
                            left_rem,
                            right_rem, expr)
                expr.pop()

        recurse(s, 0, 0, 0, left, right, [])
        return list(result.keys())
